@page "/graph-exploring"

<CatHead Title="Exploring a graph">
    Everything a reader does with a graph that is already drawn: narrowing it to what matters, finding a
    record, following its links and reading one in full. Every control here is ordinary markup the app
    writes &mdash; a chip, a select, a search field, a menu, a panel &mdash; with a <code>data-graph-*</code>
    attribute, and none of it needs a line of the app's own script. The markup of a graph itself is on
    <a href="/graph">Graph</a>.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-information-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Which graph a control drives</span>
        The graph named by <code>data-graph-for</code> on the control or on an element around it, else the
        graph the control is inside, else the one in its <code>[data-graph-frame]</code>. So a toolbar, a legend
        and a panel sit anywhere in the frame and find their graph. A toggle button's
        <code>aria-pressed</code> is flipped by the script; a checkbox, a radio, a select and a text field keep
        their native state, which a Blazor binding can own. <code>data-graph-action="reset"</code> puts every
        control back as it was first drawn.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.LegendChips" Title="Filter chips that are the legend" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; switch a kind off.</strong> A <code>button.chip</code> with
        <code>data-graph-filter="kind"</code> and a <code>value</code> is the key and the switch at once, so there
        is no second legend to keep in step. Its <code>.graph-swatch</code> wears the <code>.series-*</code> class
        of the kind's tone and the modifier of its shape &mdash; <code>--square</code>, <code>--diamond</code>,
        <code>--triangle</code>, <code>--hexagon</code> &mdash; and a chip that is off <strong>hides its
        value</strong>. A <code>button.chip</code> with <code>aria-pressed="true"</code> looks chosen by itself;
        it needs no <code>.chip--active</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>data-graph-stats</code> keeps a sentence current in the app's words. <code>.graph-empty--filtered</code>
        shows when the filters hide every record, and a <code>reset</code> button in it puts every control back.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.ChooseOne" Title="Choosing one value" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> A <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> or a group of radios with <code>data-graph-filter</code>
        <strong>chooses</strong>: only records with that value stay, and <code>value=""</code> chooses all. A record
        without the field is not what was chosen, so it goes &mdash; the gateway and the message bus, which no team
        owns, leave as soon as a team is chosen. A value with spaces chooses each of its words: &ldquo;Needs
        attention&rdquo; is <code>value="degraded down"</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        That is the difference from a chip, which is a switch and hides only what it names. Choose Platform and
        Needs attention together to see the empty state say so in the app's words.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.MutedAndIsolated" Title="Muted and unlinked records" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>data-muted</code> draws a record quieter &mdash; retired, archived, out of
        scope &mdash; and <code>data-graph-show="muted"</code> on a checkbox or a switch shows or hides all of them.
        <code>data-graph-show="isolated"</code> shows the records with no visible link, and while it is off they go
        with whatever took their last link away: hide the retired applications and the receipt archive, fed only
        by paper forms, goes too.
    </p>
    <p>
        Both start as their control says, so this graph opens without the three applications that stand alone.
        <code>.graph-swatch--muted</code> is the key for a quieter record.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.LinkFilters" Title="Filtering links" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>data-graph-filter="edge.kind"</code> filters links by their
        <code>data-kind</code>. A link's key is a <code>.graph-line</code> &mdash; <code>--dashed</code>,
        <code>--dotted</code>, <code>--light</code>, <code>--heavy</code>, <code>--arrow</code> &mdash; in the
        <code>.series-*</code> colour its <code>data-tone</code> paints, with the same <code>data-line</code> and
        <code>data-weight</code> on the links, so a line style carries the difference as well as the colour.
    </p>
    <p>
        A chip written <code>aria-pressed="false"</code> starts off: monitoring stays hidden until it is asked for.
        A record keeps its place when its links go; untick <code>data-graph-show="isolated"</code> beside the chips
        to take away what is left with none.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.Search" Title="Search" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; try &ldquo;refund&rdquo;, then &ldquo;login code&rdquo;.</strong> A text field with
        <code>data-graph-search</code> marks records as it is typed in, and once typing pauses the view settles on
        the matches and what they link to. It marks rather than hides, because where a record sits among the others
        is what a graph is for. Every word has to appear in a record's name, its id, its <code>data-meta</code>, its
        kind or its <code>data-tags</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>data-graph-stats-match</code> replaces the stats sentence while a search is marking records. The mark
        is a ring in the accent colour, so give the records of a graph that is searched tones that are not close to
        it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.Neighbourhood" Title="A record's neighbourhood" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; reach two steps out, then right-click http-client.</strong> A neighbourhood is one
        record and what it touches, and nothing else. <code>data-graph-focus</code> starts the graph on one, and
        <code>data-graph-focus-depth</code> or a <code>data-graph-option="depth"</code> control says how many links
        out to reach. The <code>focus</code> action moves to another record &mdash; from the menu, the side panel or
        a button &mdash; and <code>unfocus</code> shows everything again.
    </p>
    <p>
        The record in focus is ringed and never filtered away, and the filters still apply around it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.SidePanel" Title="The side panel" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; select a service.</strong> The panel is what the canvas cannot be: text, and controls a
        keyboard and a screen reader reach. <code>[data-graph-detail]</code> is shown while a record is selected and
        hidden while none is. Every <code>[data-graph-field]</code> in it takes that field of the record as text
        &mdash; a <code>data-*</code> of the record's own names one, as <code>team</code>, <code>owner</code> and
        <code>host</code> do here &mdash; and <code>[data-graph-icon]</code> takes its icon. A field the record does
        not have is hidden.
    </p>
    <p>
        The neighbours are rows cloned from <code>&lt;template data-graph-neighbour&gt;</code> into
        <code>[data-graph-neighbours]</code>, one per linked record. <code>[data-graph-link]</code> takes the label of
        the link, and the row gets <code>data-graph-direction</code>, which shows one of
        <code>.graph-direction-out</code>, <code>.graph-direction-in</code> and <code>.graph-direction-both</code>. A
        <code>data-graph-action</code> in a row acts on that neighbour: <code>select</code> moves the panel there and
        <code>focus</code> shows its neighbourhood.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.graph-detail</code> on a <code>.card.card--fill</code> in a <code>.sedna-split-aside</code> is as tall
        as the graph: its head and foot stay and its body scrolls. <code>--graph-height</code> on the
        <code>.graph-frame</code> sizes the graph and the panel together. From the keyboard, the arrows move between
        records and <kbd class="kbd">Space</kbd> selects, and the <code>[data-graph-live]</code> region says each in
        the sentence of <code>data-graph-announce</code> or <code>data-graph-announce-select</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.DefaultTip" Title="The default tooltip" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; point at a step.</strong> With nothing written, pointing at a record, or reaching it
        with the arrow keys, shows the library's <a href="/hover-hints">hover hint</a> &mdash; the bubble every
        <code>data-tip</code> uses &mdash; above it, with the record's name and its <code>data-meta</code>. A link
        says its label. That is enough for most graphs: write a tooltip of your own when a record has more to say.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.Tooltip" Title="A tooltip of your own" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; point at a device, then at a link.</strong> <code>.graph-tip</code> with
        <code>data-graph-tip</code>, written once inside the graph and filled for each record:
        <code>.graph-tip-head</code> holds the <code>[data-graph-icon]</code> and the <code>.graph-tip-title</code>,
        <code>.graph-tip-meta</code> is a quieter line, and <code>.graph-tip-ref</code> is an identifier &mdash; an
        address, a host name &mdash; in the monospace face. Any field fills a slot, and one a record lacks is hidden:
        the upstream provider has no rack.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>data-graph-tip="edge"</code> is the tooltip for links, here with the link's label and its
        <code>data-speed</code>. The script fills both with text and places them inside the frame, clear of the
        pointer; the words, the order and the look are the app's.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.ContextMenu" Title="The context menu" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; right-click a page, or the empty canvas.</strong> <code>.menu.graph-menu</code> with
        <code>data-graph-menu</code>, inside the graph, opens where a right click, a long press or the context-menu
        key lands. It is the library's <a href="/menu"><code>.menu</code></a> with the app's items. A
        <code>data-graph-action</code> item acts on the record the menu was opened on, and an item that does not
        apply hides itself: <code>open</code> on a record with no <code>data-href</code>, every record's item on the
        background. A <code>.menu-label</code> with <code>data-graph-field="label"</code> names the record.
    </p>
    <p>
        Here <code>open</code> goes to the page, <code>hide</code> takes one record away, and <code>show-all</code>
        brings back every record hidden that way. The arrow keys move through the items, and
        <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> closes the menu and puts focus back on the graph.
        <code>sedna-graph-context</code> is dispatched first and is cancelable, for an app that opens something of its
        own instead.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.ColourBy" Title="Colour by" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>data-tone</code> is the colouring a graph starts in; <code>data-tone-region</code>
        and <code>data-tone-status</code> on the same records are two more, and a <code>data-graph-option="colour"</code>
        control switches between them &mdash; <code>tone</code>, or the name after <code>data-tone-</code>. Every tone
        is a <code>.series-*</code> colour, so the keys are the canvas's own in every theme and colour-vision setting.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each colouring's keys sit beside its radio, so the legend in force is the one that is chosen, with no script
        to swap legends. The shape stays with the kind whatever the colour says.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.Names" Title="Names" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>data-graph-option="labels"</code>: <code>auto</code> names what there is room
        for, the way a map does &mdash; what is pointed at, selected or found first, then <code>data-hub</code>
        records, then the busiest &mdash; and leaves out a name that would run into one already placed. Zooming in
        makes room, so more appear. <code>all</code> names every record; <code>none</code> only the one pointed at,
        selected or found.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>data-graph-option="edge-labels"</code>: <code>hover</code>, the default for dots, names a link while
        one of its records is lit; <code>always</code> writes every link's name, which suits boxes better than a map
        of dots; <code>none</code> never does. Both options are also attributes on the graph,
        <code>data-graph-labels</code> and <code>data-graph-edge-labels</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.OpenOnClick" Title="Opening a record" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; this one navigates.</strong> A double click, or <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> on the
        record the keyboard is on, opens it: <code>sedna-graph-open</code> is dispatched and, unless it is cancelled,
        the record's <code>data-href</code> is followed through a real link, so Blazor's router and any other
        client-side router handle it. <code>data-graph-open="tap"</code> opens on a single click instead, for a graph
        that is a way around, like this map of the graph pages; <code>none</code> never opens.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphExploring.Explorer" Title="All of it together" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> One frame: search, a team select, the kinds as chips, the stats and reset in the
        toolbar; zoom, fit and full screen on the canvas, with <code>.graph-tools-sep</code> between the groups and
        <code>.graph-when-off</code> and <code>.graph-when-on</code> for the two icons of the full-screen toggle; a
        tooltip, a menu and a live region inside the graph; the panel beside it. Full screen takes the whole
        <code>.graph-frame</code>, so the toolbar and the panel come with it. Not one line of it is script.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="controls">Controls <CatAnchor Id="controls" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Attribute</th><th>On</th><th>Does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-filter="field"</code></td><td>a chip</td><td>A checkbox, or a <code>button</code> with <code>aria-pressed</code>. Hides records with its <code>value</code> while it is off.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-filter="field"</code></td><td>a select, radios</td><td>Chooses: only records with the value stay. <code>""</code> chooses all.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-filter="edge.kind"</code></td><td>either</td><td>The same, for links.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-show="muted"</code>, <code>"isolated"</code></td><td>a checkbox, a switch, a toggle</td><td>Shows muted records, and records with no visible link.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-search</code></td><td>a text field</td><td>Marks the records that match what is typed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-option="…"</code></td><td>a select, radios</td><td><code>labels</code>, <code>edge-labels</code>, <code>colour</code>, <code>depth</code>, and <code>layout</code>, <code>direction</code>, <code>spacing</code>, <code>nodes</code>, <code>curve</code>, <code>arrows</code>, which are on <a href="/graph-layouts">Graph layouts</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-stats</code></td><td>any element</td><td>A sentence kept current: <code>{nodes}</code>, <code>{edges}</code>, <code>{matches}</code>, <code>{totalNodes}</code>, <code>{totalEdges}</code>. <code>data-graph-stats-match</code> replaces it while a search marks records.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-action="…"</code></td><td>a button</td><td><code>select</code>, <code>focus</code>, <code>unfocus</code>, <code>open</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>hide</code>, <code>show-all</code>, <code>reset</code>, <code>zoom-in</code>, <code>zoom-out</code>, <code>fit</code>, <code>fullscreen</code>. An action on one record takes the button's <code>value</code>, else the menu's record, else the selection. The rest are on <a href="/graph-plugins">Graph plugins</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-for="id"</code></td><td>a control, or an element around several</td><td>Names the graph they drive.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="filling">Markup the script fills <CatAnchor Id="filling" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Where</th><th>Gets</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-field="name"</code></td><td>the tooltip, the menu, the panel, a row</td><td>That field of the record, as text. Hidden when the record has none.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-icon</code></td><td>the same</td><td>The record's icon class, added to the element's own.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-tone</code></td><td>written on the tooltip and the panel</td><td>The record's tone, for the app's own styling.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-tip</code>, <code>data-graph-tip="edge"</code></td><td>inside the graph</td><td>The tooltip for records, and for links.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-menu</code></td><td>a <code>.menu</code> inside the graph</td><td>Opened on a record, or on the background.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-detail</code></td><td>in the frame, or with <code>data-graph-for</code></td><td>Shown while a record is selected.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-neighbours</code>, <code>&lt;template data-graph-neighbour&gt;</code></td><td>in the panel</td><td>One row per linked record, with <code>data-graph-direction</code> and a <code>[data-graph-link]</code> for the link's label.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-graph-live</code></td><td>a polite live region</td><td>The sentence in <code>data-graph-announce</code> for each record the keyboard reaches, and in <code>data-graph-announce-select</code> for a selection: <code>{label}</code>, <code>{kind}</code>, <code>{meta}</code>, any field, and <code>{links}</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    A Blazor app that renders its own panel from <code>@@onsedna-graph-select</code> leaves
    <code>data-graph-detail</code> off; <a href="/graph-blazor">Graph in Blazor</a> shows it.
</p>

<h2 id="keyboard">Keyboard <CatAnchor Id="keyboard" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Key</th><th>Pointer</th><th>Does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">←</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">↑</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">→</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">↓</kbd></td><td>hover</td><td>The nearest record that way: lit, named, and said.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Page Down</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">Page Up</kbd></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>The next or previous record linked to this one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Home</kbd></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>The root, else the most connected record.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Space</kbd></td><td>click</td><td>Select, or clear the selection.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd></td><td>double click</td><td>Open.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">F10</kbd>, the menu key</td><td>right click, long press</td><td>The menu.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> + arrows, <kbd class="kbd">+</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">−</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">0</kbd></td><td>drag, wheel</td><td>Pan, zoom, fit.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd></td><td>click the background</td><td>Clear the selection, then the ring.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="events">Events <CatAnchor Id="events" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Event</th><th>Detail</th><th></th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>sedna-graph-select</code></td><td>the record, or <code>{ id: null }</code></td><td></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sedna-graph-hover</code></td><td>the record</td><td>Pointed at, or reached by keyboard.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sedna-graph-open</code></td><td>the record</td><td>Cancelable: cancelled, <code>data-href</code> is not followed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sedna-graph-context</code></td><td>the record, with <code>x</code> and <code>y</code></td><td>Cancelable: cancelled, the app's menu does not open.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sedna-graph-change</code></td><td>the stats</td><td>After a filter, a search, new data or a fold.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    They bubble from the <code>[data-graph]</code> element. A record is
    <code>{ id, label, kind, group, cluster, parent, meta, href, tone, tags, fields, keyboard }</code>.
</p>
